**Read:** [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the complete non-silicate/economic-mineral sections and descriptive references; [Handbook of Mineralogy — Anthony, Bideaux, Bladh, and Nichols](../sources/Handbook-of-Mineralogy.lingua#r_10TEF3RSN7XFMKTGYGY4WR3P85), complete selected entries and their primary references; [Mineral names and classification reports — IMA-CNMNC](../sources/IMA-CNMNC-Mineral-Names-and-Reports.lingua#r_1B4ZWGSZKGP6JM3NKPY0D5RJXH), the relevant full class/group reports.

Carbonates, oxides/hydroxides, sulfides/sulfosalts, sulfates, phosphates, halides, borates, native elements, and other classes connect distinctive chemical groups with diverse structures. Study the full non-silicate descriptions rather than equating mineralogy with only the silicate rock-formers.

Compare calcite/dolomite, iron oxides and hydroxides, common sulfides, gypsum/anhydrite, apatite, halite, and representative additional classes. Preserve oxidation state, hydration, substitutions, polymorphism, and association. A native-metal grain, sulfide-hosted metal, and trace substitution can require very different characterization or processing despite containing the same element.

Physical, optical, diffraction, spectral, and chemical observations constrain different candidates. Opaque minerals require appropriate reflected-light or analytical methods; absence of transmitted light does not identify a species. Weathering can produce surface coatings or replacements that differ from the grain interior.

**Work:** compare full structure/composition/property and occurrence accounts for a mineral from at least five classes, including one opaque and one hydrated example. Explain the discriminating observations and one misleading shortcut for each. Reconstruct a weathering or replacement association with explicit component transfers and alternative interpretations.

Read all non-silicate chapters/entries in the book and selected complete references. Later ore, sedimentary, industrial-mineral, and waste questions will deepen particular classes without making their general foundations a separate repeated introduction in every material trail.

**Continue:** [Investigate small phases whose information exceeds their volume fraction](Accessory-minerals-inclusions-and-disproportionate-information.lingua#r_7X3R4TNR1VE7QAY8F44QD40JPY).

